# Production Operations & Scaling

## Environment separation
Maintain isolated local/development/staging/production configuration and credentials. Never use production secrets in development or commit environment secrets.

## Deployment
Production deployments must be reproducible, auditable and reversible.
Required before release:
- CI green;
- migration safety reviewed;
- backup verified;
- health/readiness checks;
- smoke tests;
- provider sandbox/E2E validation where applicable;
- observability and alerts;
- rollback procedure.

Prefer backward-compatible schema migrations: expand -> deploy compatible code -> migrate/backfill -> contract -> remove obsolete structures later.

## Realtime deployment
Treat Asterisk, SIP Edge and Pipecat as operationally distinct components with explicit health checks, capacity limits and failure isolation. Do not let API deploys silently break the media path.

## Observability
Track at minimum:
- API latency/error rate;
- database health/query latency;
- queue depth/job latency/failure rate;
- payment success/failure/webhook lag;
- commission reconciliation mismatches;
- payout aging/failure;
- KYC queue aging;
- number provisioning failures;
- call setup/answer/completion/failure rates;
- realtime latency and provider errors;
- transfer success/failure;
- knowledge ingestion failures;
- integration/webhook delivery failures;
- CPU/memory/network and service saturation.

Use correlation/request IDs across HTTP -> async jobs -> provider calls -> call lifecycle.

## Alerts
Alert on symptoms and business impact, not only infrastructure. Financial mismatches, duplicate settlement attempts, failed payouts, tenant isolation errors and call-routing failures are high severity.

## Backups & recovery
Persistent data/configuration requires automated backups and tested restoration. Document RPO/RTO assumptions. A backup that has never been restored is not considered verified.

## Incidents
For incidents: contain -> preserve evidence -> identify blast radius -> mitigate -> verify recovery -> communicate -> root-cause analysis -> corrective action. Never hide an incident by destructive cleanup.

## Scaling
Scale horizontally where possible. Separate API, background workers, realtime voice workers and provider-facing workloads when load characteristics differ. Introduce read replicas, caching, partitioning or service extraction only with measured evidence and an ADR.

## Capacity planning
For every scale change estimate:
- concurrent calls;
- calls/minute;
- audio bandwidth;
- STT/TTS/LLM request rates;
- database writes/second;
- queue throughput;
- webhook throughput;
- provider rate limits;
- storage growth for recordings/transcripts/knowledge;
- tenant hot-spot risk.

## Post-deployment verification
After every production deployment verify health, error rates, critical workflows, call routing, payment/webhook processing and queue health before declaring success.
